%I A399960 #55 Nov 15 2026 16:16:17
%S A399960 4,6,198,348
%N A399960 Terms m of A014574 such that there are no two distinct terms x < y of A014574 with x + y = 2*m.
%C A399960 An integer m is a twin prime middle if m-1 and m+1 are both prime. For any twin middle m, the even number 2m = (m-1)+(m+1) is a sum of two primes. This sequence lists the twin middles m for which 2m cannot be written as m1 + m2 with two other distinct twin middles m1 < m2.
%C A399960 Corresponds to the zeros in A359556.
%C A399960 No further terms exist up to 2*10^8.
%C A399960 It is conjectured that the sequence is finite. A proof that there are no terms beyond 348 would imply the twin prime conjecture.
%H A399960 Mourad Belafsahi, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23727386">A Symmetry Conjecture for Twin Prime Middles and Its Relation to the Twin Prime Conjecture</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A399960 For m = 198, the corresponding twin prime pair is (197, 199). The value 2m = 396 cannot be expressed as the sum of any other two twin prime middles, making m = 198 an element of this sequence.
%e A399960 The last known exception is m = 348 (2m = 696), where the twin pair is (347, 349).
%o A399960 (Python)
%o A399960 from sympy import isprime
%o A399960 T = [m for m in range(4, 1000) if isprime(m-1) and isprime(m+1)]
%o A399960 S = set(T)
%o A399960 print([m for m in T if m < 500 and not any((m-d) in S and (m+d) in S for d in range(1, m))])
%o A399960 (PARI) isavg(k) = isprime(k-1) && isprime(k+1); \\ A014574
%o A399960 isok(k) = if (isavg(k), for (i=1, k, if (isavg(k-i) && isavg(k+i), return(0));); 1;); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Oct 30 2026
%Y A399960 Subsequence of A014574.
%Y A399960 Cf. A359556.
%K A399960 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A399960 1,1
%A A399960 _Mourad Belafsahi_, Oct 27 2026
